open_questions:
  - "Was the marriage between Anna Maud and Robert Davis already strained at the time of their son Robert Lincoln Davis's death in 1918? Family memory connects his death to the divorce two years later, but no documentation establishes when the marriage's difficulties began."
  - "What is the relationship, if any, between the 'Simon T. Davis' of the 1870 census and the 'Davis Peter' headship recorded for the same family in the 1880 census? Census and death-certificate evidence together support 'Simon Peter Davis' as the father's full name, recorded inconsistently by different enumerators, but this has not been confirmed by an independent record naming him in full."
  - "Is Arthur M. Davis (Anna Maud and Robert's son, b. 1908) named for Robert's younger brother of the same name (b. ca. 1868, Michigan)? The naming echo is suggestive but unconfirmed."
  - "Anna Maud's 1954 obituary states she 'came to Creede' in 1915, but the site's working timeline (corroborated by the 1921 Creede Candle wedding announcement on Alice Josephine's page) places the family's move to Creede in 1918. Worth resolving which date is accurate, or whether these describe two different events (e.g., an earlier visit versus a permanent move)."
  - "What documentation exists, if any, for Anna Maud's adoption of Raymond Lane (Charles Lane's son by a prior marriage)? Family account (via Charlotte Lane McClure) and the 1930 census enumeration as 'adopted son' are the only sources at present — no formal adoption record has been located."
  - "Edna Rosamond Davis appears, per Find a Grave and her mother's 1954 obituary, to have married a man surnamed Cook and died in 1971 in or near Altadena, California — but this rests on two secondary sources pointing the same direction, not yet a primary record. A marriage license, death certificate, or SSDI entry would confirm it."
  - "Robert Lincoln Davis's age at death is given as 18 by the contemporary 1918 newspaper notice but as 17 by Find a Grave and his documented birth date (29 Dec 1900). Not resolved."
  - "The Creede Candle's 11 September 1920 marriage notice describes Anna Maud and Charles Lane's wedding as having taken place 'on Wednesday,' which would be 8 September rather than the 9th currently recorded for this marriage. Not yet resolved."
  - "No published version of Anna Maud's 1954 obituary has been located. The surviving document is a handwritten draft, prepared by her daughters Jo Jackson and Charlotte McClure for submission to a newspaper — it may never have run, or ran in a paper and date not yet identified."
